CODE CITATION — EXACT MATCH. drbd_state.c (9.3.2, both /tmp/drbdsrc and /home/tommy/projects/drbdsrc_clone at identical HEAD a46cbd9; function byte-identical in both trees):

  6379  bool drbd_data_accessible(struct drbd_device *device, enum which_state which)
  6380  {
  6381          struct drbd_peer_device *peer_device;
  6382          bool data_accessible = false;
  6383
  6384          if (device->disk_state[which] == D_UP_TO_DATE)
  6385                  return true;
  6386
  6387          rcu_read_lock();
  6388          for_each_peer_device_rcu(peer_device, device) {
  6389                  struct net_conf *nc;
  6390                  nc = rcu_dereference(peer_device->connection->transport.net_conf);
  6391                  if (nc && !nc->allow_remote_read)
  6392                          continue;
  6393                  if (peer_device->disk_state[which] == D_UP_TO_DATE) {
  6394                          data_accessible = true;
  6395                          break;
  6396                  }

Lines 6384-6385 are quoted verbatim by the claim and are EXACT. The local-disk D_UP_TO_DATE check (6384-6385, returns true) precedes the peer loop (6387-6397). allow_remote_read (nc->allow_remote_read, 6391) is read ONLY inside that loop. Therefore when the local disk is UpToDate the function returns true at 6385 and allow_remote_read is never evaluated.

"THE ARM" = the new-current-UUID rotation flag (__NEW_CUR_UUID), Bedrock's term, per /home/tommy/projects/Bedrock/docs/drbd-uuid-quorum-foundations.md §2 ("Stage 1 — ARM a flag"). The primary ARM gate (the lost_contact_to_peer_data path that fires in the live-captured leak) is drbd_state.c:3096-3099:
  if (lost_contact_to_peer_data(peer_disk_state)) {
      if (role[NEW] == R_PRIMARY && !test_bit(UNREGISTERED, &device->flags) &&
          drbd_data_accessible(device, NEW))
          create_new_uuid = true;
then armed at 3135-3136: if (create_new_uuid && !susp_uuid[OLD]) set_bit(__NEW_CUR_UUID, &device->flags). The same drbd_data_accessible(device, NEW) gate also appears at 3113, 3128. which_state enum (drbd_int.h:871): NOW, OLD = NOW, NEW — so the 6384 short-circuit checks the LOCAL disk for every which value (OLD/NOW/NEW alike).

WHY THE SHORT-CIRCUIT WINS IN THE LEAK SCENARIO: In the captured 2v2 partition (doc §3, kernel log lines 98-100), the losing Primary's LOCAL disk stays UpToDate; only PEER disks go "UpToDate -> DUnknown". Thus device->disk_state[NEW]==D_UP_TO_DATE, line 6384 returns true without entering the loop, and allow_remote_read is never consulted. ARM fires regardless of allow_remote_read.

ADVERSARIAL REFUTATION ATTEMPTS — ALL FAIL:
1) Could allow_remote_read=no force local disk off UpToDate? No. drbd.conf-9.0 manpage: "allow-remote-read ... Allows or disallows DRBD to read FROM A PEER node ... When the disk of a primary node is DETACHED, DRBD will try to continue reading ... from another node." It governs only this node's reads from peers; it has no effect on local disk_state. A Primary with a healthy backing device is D_UP_TO_DATE independent of the option (default yes, since 9.0.19).
2) Could allow_remote_read disarm via the OTHER use site (quorum exclusion, drbd_state.c:1421 / 1490 "Excluding from quorum calculation because allow-remote-read = no")? That path alters have_quorum, which gates Stage-2 EXECUTE on the write route (drbd_sender.c:3443 `if (device->have_quorum[NOW] && drbd_data_accessible(...))`) and the disconnect route's PRIMARY_LOST_QUORUM guard (drbd_receiver.c:9886) — but NOT Stage-1 ARM at 3096-3099, whose only data-reachability gate is drbd_data_accessible, not have_quorum. So allow_remote_read cannot change whether the flag is ARMED.
3) The actual leak EXECUTE path (doc-identified susp_uuid false->true edge, drbd_state.c:4466-4471) reads only the NEW_CUR_UUID flag and calls drbd_uuid_new_current(); it never consults allow_remote_read either.
4) cached_err_io / on-no-data-accessible (the io-error "arming" at drbd_state.c:898, consumed at drbd_req.c:670 + drbd_int.h:2903) is ALSO gated by the same drbd_data_accessible function with the same 6384 short-circuit, so the conclusion holds for that interpretation of "ARM" too (manpage on-no-data-accessible default io-error: "System calls fail with errno set to EIO").

Line numbers in the claim (6384-6385) are exact; sibling helper calc_data_accessible (6337-6367) has the identical structure ("By intention", per comment 6402) with the same local-first short-circuit at 6344-6345. No DRBD path makes allow_remote_read relevant to disarming the ARM when the local disk is UpToDate.
